.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-collection-add 1  "tapectl-collection-add " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-collection\-add \- Add a collection: a folder whose subfolders are each a unit
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl collection add\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] <\fB\-\-tenant\fR> [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-unit\-depth\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-archive\-set\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-dotfiles\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> <\fIROOT\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Add a collection: a folder whose subfolders are each a unit
.PP
Each unit folder becomes a unit of one tenant. ROOT must be an absolute path to a directory that exists, neither inside another collection\*(Aqs root nor holding one. The tenant and the archive set must exist. The user tapectl runs as (the service user, under tapectl\-op) must be able to read the whole tree and, unless \-\-no\-dotfiles, write a .tapectl\-unit.toml into each unit folder; when it cannot, the setfacl lines that grant it are printed and nothing is added, since it cannot grant them itself. Then the [[collections]] table is written into config.toml, its comments kept, and the sync dry run is shown: `tapectl collection sync` registers the units.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = added (with \-\-dry\-run: it would be); 2 = refused, nothing written.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-tenant\fR \fI<TENANT>\fR
The tenant the collection\*(Aqs units are registered under
.TP
\fB\-\-unit\-depth\fR \fI<UNIT_DEPTH>\fR [default: 1]
How deep the unit folders are: 1 = ROOT\*(Aqs subfolders, 2 = theirs

2 is for a ROOT/show/season layout, where each season is a unit.
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fI<EXCLUDE>\fR
A folder or file name to leave out, as a glob, e.g. \*(Aq*.partial\*(Aq

Repeat it for several.
.TP
\fB\-\-archive\-set\fR \fI<ARCHIVE_SET>\fR
The archive set the collection\*(Aqs units are registered in
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dotfiles\fR
Write no .tapectl\-unit.toml: each unit is known by its path

For a source tapectl must not write. A renamed folder then reads as a new unit, and the old one as missing.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
The collection\*(Aqs name, which begins each unit\*(Aqs name: NAME/FOLDER
.TP
<\fIROOT\fR>
The folder to walk: an absolute path
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.SH EXTRA
EXAMPLES:
  One folder per unit, each a unit of the tenant family:
    tapectl collection add photos /srv/photos \-\-tenant family

  A show/season layout, each season a unit, partial downloads left out:
    tapectl collection add tv /srv/tv \-\-tenant family \-\-unit\-depth 2 \-\-exclude \*(Aq*.partial\*(Aq

  See what it would add first:
    tapectl collection add photos /srv/photos \-\-tenant family \-\-dry\-run

  Then register the units:
    tapectl collection sync
